Scenario 2 - API Performance

The main performance issue was caused by an N+1 query problem in the orders endpoint.

The controller was initially loading all orders under Order::all(), after which the resource accessed the related customer
and items relationships for each individual order. This resulted in each relationship being lazy loaded separately.

Using the debugbar, I confirmed that the endpoint was retrieving approximately 7000 model instances across 2001 db queries.
The request was using around 18MB of memory and took appox 300ms to complete.

I also reviewed the associated orders, customers and order_items tables and identified that some of the relations columns
were missing appropriate db indexes, which could further impact query performance as the dataset grows.

The primary optimisation was to replace the unbounded Order::all() query with pagination and eager loading:

```
Order::with(['customer', 'items'])->paginate(50);
```

This addressed two separate issues:
1) Pagination prevents the api from loading the complete orders dataset into memory for every request (which on much
larger datasets could cause memory exhaustion issues resulting in platform slowdowns and/or downtime)
2) Eager loading retrieved the required relations in a small, fixed number of queries rather than issuing additional queries
for each individual order.

With pagination set to 50 records, the endpoint now requires only a small number of queries for each request: on
pagination count query, one query for the orders, one for the related customers and one for the related order items.

I also updated the API resources so that the related data is handled through dedicated resources and whenLoaded(), ensuring
relations are only serialised when they have been explicitly loaded.

DB indexes where added where appropriate to improve the performance of relationship lookups (worth nothing here that in
a production environment depending on the size of the table this is something that would need a migration or potentially need to
be down OOHs in order to avoid issues with the table locking up)

Caching was considered and initially implemented, but i removed it from the final solution. A production caching implementation
would ideally use a shared caches such as Redis and introduce cache invalidation considerations. This felt outside the scope of the
scenario as the underlying query and pagination improvements addressed the fundamental performance problems directly.

During implementation, I also reviewed the generated changes rather than accepting them without modification. An unrelated
change introduction by the LLM was reverted.

Finally i ran the test suite again and confirmed that all tests passed after the performance changes.